AI is coming for the world of competitive Excel

Or is it?

BY SHIKHAR SACHDEV (https://x.com/shikhar_sachdev?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7=
A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-Hzq=
M5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 )

Michael Jarman froze. In his first five minutes at the HyperX Esports Arena=
 in Las Vegas, he forgot everything he knew about Microsoft Excel.

This was problematic, because Jarman was participating in the 2023 Microsof=
t Excel World Championship (MEWC). The arena was packed with hundreds of fa=
ns (with millions watching the YouTube (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DU=
DGdPE_C9u8&t=3D39s&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBB=
dRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 ) s=
tream), all screaming their favorite competitor=E2=80=99s name, egging them=
 on to solve the creative puzzle =E2=80=94 known as a case =E2=80=94 broadc=
ast on the big screen. Every five minutes, the last ranked competitor would=
 be eliminated, until only one remained.

As things stood at the outset of the event, it was Jarman, feeling pressure=
d by the big stage and making silly mistakes, who was ready to be axed.

=E2=80=9CWhen your spreadsheet isn=E2=80=99t working, that=E2=80=99s when i=
t gets very stressful that thousands of people are watching you," he told T=
he Hustle.

Semi-finalists focus on their cases at last year=E2=80=99s championships. (=
Courtesy of the Microsoft Excel World Championship)

Competitive Excel is like Formula One: Top athletes keep it light off the c=
ircuit, often sharing learnings and grabbing dinner when in town. But this =
camaraderie doesn=E2=80=99t prevent a burning desire to drive each other of=
f the track when a golden belt is at stake.

Luckily for Jarman, he wasn=E2=80=99t the only one who froze. He settled do=
wn and finished second at the competition. Over the next few months, hunche=
d at a laptop even while on holiday, he prepared for the 2024 world champio=
nship. He wanted that darn belt.

This year, with the 2025 Microsoft Excel World Championships set to take pl=
ace in December, Jarman has to worry about more than Excel guys. A new cont=
ender threatens to spell the end for not just professional Excel competitor=
s but also office workers who specialize in Excel in millions of offices ac=
ross the world.

It=E2=80=99s AI. And, unlike Jarman, it doesn=E2=80=99t tend to freeze.

The weird and wonderful world of competitive Excel

Elite athletes hate losing. The world of competitive Microsoft Excel is no =
different. Just ask Jarman=E2=80=99s wife.

On their second date, at a boardgame cafe, Jarman told her that =E2=80=9Che=
 wouldn=E2=80=99t just let her win to be nice.=E2=80=9D And he didn=E2=80=
=99t.

It was at university where Jarman, who=E2=80=99s now 30 and lives in Toront=
o, first developed a reputation as an Excel savant. Then, while working as =
an analyst at the project finance firm Operis, his daily life revolved arou=
nd the software. He accumulated thousands of hours tinkering with spreadshe=
ets, climbing to a director position at Operis.

One of his bosses suggested he check out the competitive Microsoft Excel sp=
ace, a deeply niche set of tournaments focused on solving real-life financi=
al modeling cases, such as forecasting the amount of money fans might spend=
 at a tournament or building a three-statement model for a manufacturing co=
mpany. Jarman would have some initial success, winning ModelOff (https://fm=
worldcup.com/history/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkN=
oBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 =
) in 2018.

Michael Jarman strolls through the hype tunnel in seasonally appropriate Ex=
cel attire. (Courtesy of the Microsoft Excel World Championship)

Over time, the contests would evolve into a full-blown eSport: The Microsof=
t Excel World Championship, launched in 2021, focuses on solving cases that=
 are =E2=80=9Csexier=E2=80=9D than, say, modeling debt.

Sample cases at the MEWC include ones like =E2=80=9CLana banana (https://ww=
w.youtube.com/watch?v=3DSjUglZr81x4&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8L=
M4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsm=
i=3D391130758 ) ,=E2=80=9D where one must write formulas to help an ice-ska=
ting monkey named Lana collect as many bananas as possible, beginning on th=
e starting cell. Because Lana is said to be standing on a steep hill, she c=
an only move down, diagonally left-down and diagonally right-down.

Jarman has competed in every MEWC. After back-to-back podium finishes in 20=
22 and 2023, he got an early lead in 2024, racking up 100 points for modeli=
ng the stats of World of Warcraft-themed characters to help them progress t=
hrough different levels. Three-time consecutive world champion Andrew Ngai =
was following closely behind.

Halfway through, the two were tied at 705 points. =E2=80=9CThis is crazy, t=
his is crazy,=E2=80=9D the commentators exclaimed.

Curiously, between rounds, Microsoft showed off Copilot, its AI companion f=
or Excel. The contestants found this funny because Copilot only seemed to b=
e answering the easy questions.

But was this really a laughing matter?

Sufficient seniority

One of the biggest differences between a Formula One driver and an Excel Wo=
rld Champion, outside of the relative risks involved in both professions (t=
hough many will attest that a debt model can get quite dangerous), is that =
the latter still has a day job.

The day job, of course, is where one=E2=80=99s Excel apprenticeship begins.

Excel1-v1 (1) (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Dkl1eJmw295g&_hsenc=3Dp2ANq=
tz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdW=
ntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 )

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

For the longest time, listing Excel on your resum=C3=A9 meant strong job pr=
ospects. As the world=E2=80=99s most popular piece of white-collar software=
, going strong for over 40 years, its dominance has never been disputed.

This has meant that the Excel guy has always commanded the respect of his p=
eers.

Diarmuid Early, dubbed the =E2=80=9CLebron James=E2=80=9D of competitive Ex=
cel, remembers it well. When he worked at the Boston Consulting Group in 20=
08, he=E2=80=99d have a steady stream of people by his desk every Friday wa=
iting to ask him questions about Excel and peek at any custom formulas he m=
ight have handy.

Now, things appear to be changing.

Major Wall Street banks are expected (https://www.forbes.com/sites/jackkell=
y/2025/01/09/200000-wall-street-jobs-may-be-slashed-by-ai/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz=
-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWnt=
ZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 ) to slash up to 200k jobs over the ne=
xt three to five years due to AI adoption. And recent research (https://www=
.cnbc.com/2025/08/28/generative-ai-reshapes-us-job-market-stanford-study-sh=
ows-entry-level-young-workers.html?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM=
4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=
=3D391130758 ) , utilizing payroll data, estimates there has been a 13% dec=
line in entry-level jobs in the most AI-exposed fields.

Excel Athletes will descend on Vegas Dec. 1 for this year=E2=80=99s world c=
hampionship. (Courtesy of the Microsoft Excel World Championship

It seems like this surge in AI adoption might threaten the advantage of kno=
wing so much about Excel =E2=80=94 why bother walking over to someone=E2=80=
=99s desk when you can simply ask ChatGPT?

Early believes he=E2=80=99ll still have value, most importantly in bringing=
 =E2=80=9Cclarity on what model we should be building,=E2=80=9D rather than=
 =E2=80=9Cbuilding the model itself.=E2=80=9D

He considers himself to be =E2=80=9Csufficiently senior=E2=80=9D to charge =
roughly the same in a world with AI.

Jarman=E2=80=99s unconvinced, too. =E2=80=9CIt's not just about being good =
at Excel. It=E2=80=99s about a combination of being good at Excel but also =
being able to look at the spreadsheet, spot where things are wrong, and be =
able to stitch different pieces together.=E2=80=9D

=E2=80=9CI don=E2=80=99t think an AI will ever be as good as me or another =
director here [at Operus] who=E2=80=99s competent at Excel with 10-15 years=
 of experience,=E2=80=9D he adds.

But the same might not be true for everyone.

Excel monkeys

Earlier this year, the team behind the startup Shortcut AI, dubbed as a =E2=
=80=9Csuperhuman Excel agent,=E2=80=9D said (https://x.com/nicochristie/sta=
tus/1949862432077484396?ref_src=3Dtwsrc%5Etfw%7Ctwcamp%5Etweetembed%7Ctwter=
m%5E1949862432077484396%7Ctwgr%5E8d1e0a01dc6c832200011f46f301e6530641720a%7=
Ctwcon%5Es1_&ref_url=3Dhttps%3A%2F%2Ftechcrunch.com%2F2025%2F08%2F01%2Ffund=
amental-research-labs-nabs-33-million-from-prosus-to-build-ai-agents-for-mu=
ltiple-verticals%2F&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoB=
BdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 ) =
its agent could outperform a McKinsey or Goldman Sachs analyst 89.1% of the=
 time, and do it 10x faster.

This was no Microsoft Copilot. This was a tool that could do real damage. N=
ews spread, along with fears that tools like Shortcut AI could replace youn=
g analysts who lacked the skill of Excel world champions.

Jarman, though, doesn't think that it=E2=80=99s prudent to fire your first-=
year analyst class.

=E2=80=9CIf we sacked all the analysts tomorrow, the company would immediat=
ely be more profitable,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CBut that would be dumb, =
because who would then check the work of the AI in five to ten years time?=
=E2=80=9D

Competitors ham it up off-screen. (Courtesy of the Microsoft Excel World Ch=
ampionship)

Indeed, even though AI might be an upgrade on a junior analyst, there are l=
onger term implications to getting rid of newer talent. The entry-level rol=
es aren=E2=80=99t just about immediate gains; they're about identifying and=
 developing future leaders.

Further, it=E2=80=99s only by doing the =E2=80=9Cmonkey work=E2=80=9D to bu=
ild something that one can truly understand it.

=E2=80=9CUntil you have built a three-statement model for the first time, y=
ou don=E2=80=99t intuitively understand the point of what you=E2=80=99re tr=
ying to do,=E2=80=9D Jarman says.

But we don=E2=80=99t have to look too far to find an example of technology =
replacing workers: When Excel first came out in the 1980s, a Morgan Stanley=
 (https://www.spglobal.com/en/research-insights/special-reports/look-forwar=
d/can-generative-ai-create-a-productivity-boom?utm_source=3Dchatgpt.com&_hs=
enc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2=
BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 ) analysis using BLS data=
 found that the number of Americans employed as bookkeepers and accounting =
clerks fell from about 2m to 1.5m between 1987 and 2002.

However, more analytical roles like those of financial managers and managem=
ent analysts went up (from 600k to 1.5m) over that same time period.

In other words, Excel didn=E2=80=99t eliminate jobs so much as create deman=
d for new ones.

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

Under this view, a leap in AI might just raise the bar: clients will expect=
 more complex models, longer decks, and deeper analysis.

=E2=80=9CThere are probably more people working on analyzing data now than =
there were 40 years ago, even though one person can do what a team of 50 co=
uld do back then,=E2=80=9D Early says.

In a similar scenario, AI might augment rather than automate. But there=E2=
=80=99s no guarantee that it doesn=E2=80=99t get worse before it gets bette=
r.

The next formula

As the 2024 Excel championship neared its end, Jarman anxiously watched the=
 countdown timer on a big screen. With about 15 seconds to go, his opponent=
, Ngai, threw a hail mary, using the RANDBETWEEN function to attempt an edu=
cated guess that could score the 50 points he needed to catch up to Jarman.

It didn=E2=80=99t work. The belt went from Australia to Canada, from Ngai t=
o Jarman.

When the clock reached zero seconds, Jarman leaped out of his chair. You co=
uld hear his huge sigh of relief, immediately followed by a triumphant =E2=
=80=9CCOME ON!=E2=80=9D

The belt was finally his.

The same tournament where Microsoft had shown off its AI assistant was stil=
l, ultimately, a contest of human instinct and nerve. But the tide had turn=
ed.

Michael Jarman takes home last year=E2=80=99s championship belt. (Courtesy =
of the Microsoft Excel World Championship)

In the months since, AI tools have advanced so rapidly that the MEWC has ba=
nned (https://www.linkedin.com/posts/financial-modeling-world-cup_artificia=
l-intelligence-updates-to-the-competition-activity-7356080038317899776-lt7R=
/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNoBBdRF57WKMehm9U9Qil=
Gtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758 ) their use entirely=
. Until recently, competitors were allowed to use whatever software they li=
ked =E2=80=94 including AI =E2=80=94 because it simply wasn=E2=80=99t fast =
or accurate enough to compete with humans.

That=E2=80=99s no longer true. Shortcut recently (https://www.pymnts.com/ar=
tificial-intelligence-2/2025/is-shortcut-the-new-excel-mit-startup-behind-v=
iral-tool-thinks-so/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9xlT_VL7A5iyI7pqQ8O-8LM4qeb-fHxxsTkNo=
BBdRF57WKMehm9U9QilGtwk2BiqhwHW3WdWntZmUSb-HzqM5BtDliGQ&_hsmi=3D391130758#:=
~:text=3DChristie%2C%20who%20has%20a%20background,10%20times%20faster%20tha=
n%20humans .) put out more evidence showcasing that its agent scored over 8=
0% on cases presented at the Microsoft Excel World Championship in around 1=
0 minutes. These problems usually take a trained human an hour or more.

Confident as Jarman is about his Excel skills, he still jokes that in the f=
uture his day job might be typing in six-digit authentication codes to let =
different AIs talk to each other.

For now, though, he=E2=80=99s set to keep doing what he loves in Vegas. Tha=
t is, of course, if future Excel championships can still find enough compet=
itors in a world where their day jobs may no longer involve much traditiona=
l Excel usage at all.

But as the MEWC=E2=80=99s LinkedIn account recently put it:

=E2=80=9CJust because humans invented the car doesn=E2=80=99t make it less =
fun to run a marathon.=E2=80=9D

